WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:07.320
A political threat looms over much of the
world.

00:00:07.320 --> 00:00:11.430
Developed countries as well as many emerging
countries, as in China’s case, are beginning

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:15.920
to see the effects that demographic aging
and many public services’ unsustainability

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:20.100
can cause with increasing concern.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:24.180
Along with pensions, health is another of
the fields that generate the most uncertainties

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:26.890
and fears.

00:00:26.890 --> 00:00:31.300
An increasingly aging population and the emergence
of new and expensive medical technologies

00:00:31.300 --> 00:00:33.800
have raised some very important questions:

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:40.300
Will we be able to pay for a quality health
care system or will we be condemned to a precarious

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.750
health situation that affects our life prospects?

00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.579
Is there a successful model that can lead
the way?

00:00:45.579 --> 00:00:46.579
Are there alternatives?

00:00:46.579 --> 00:00:51.250
Well, in this video we’ll look at a system
that we may more or less like, but which we

00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:57.489
should definitely know about and take into
account throughout this debate.

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:05.309
Let's see it.

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:09.509
Check out the following graph, it shows a
country leaving the Third World and becoming

00:01:09.509 --> 00:01:14.590
one of the richest areas on the planet.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:26.429
Thirty-five years ago, Singapore and Spain
had the same per capita income, today however

00:01:26.429 --> 00:01:31.049
the former more than doubles the Spanish per
capita income.

00:01:31.049 --> 00:01:40.249
In 2015, Singapore’s per capita income was
$86,100 per year and Spain’s was just $34,750.

00:01:40.249 --> 00:01:46.549
But, even if we took countries such as Finland,
Denmark, Sweden, Germany or the United States

00:01:46.549 --> 00:01:51.789
as examples, we’d be able to see that Singapore
is much richer than any of them.

00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:56.029
However, it’s often said that Singapore’s
example isn’t good enough because their

00:01:56.029 --> 00:01:59.810
wealth essentially comes from a financial
sector that is much larger than that of any

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.389
of the aforementioned countries.

00:02:01.389 --> 00:02:06.630
That’s true, but no, that’s not the key
to this small state’s success.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.780
The weight of Singapore’s financial sector
isn’t big enough to explain such differences

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:12.430
in per capita income.

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.409
In fact, if we were to eliminate the effect
of these countries’ different financial

00:02:16.409 --> 00:02:22.800
sector sizes, Singapore would still be much
richer than all the others.

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:28.210
Because actually, both trade and manufacturing
are more important in Singapore than the financial

00:02:28.210 --> 00:02:29.210
sector.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:33.550
We’re talking about one of the largest merchandise
exporting countries in the world.

00:02:33.550 --> 00:02:37.769
For example, the proportion of electronic
material that this country exports in relation

00:02:37.769 --> 00:02:43.180
to its GDP is larger than the proportion of
anything exported by countries like Spain,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.600
Sweden, Denmark, or even Germany.

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:53.600
So, the question is: How did Singapore achieve
such a high level of prosperity?

00:02:53.600 --> 00:03:00.599
Well, if we wanted to simplify it, we could
say that Singapore is the second freest economy

00:03:00.599 --> 00:03:04.939
in the world according to the Index of Economic
Freedom published annually by the Heritage

00:03:04.939 --> 00:03:06.819
Foundation and the Wall Street Journal.

00:03:06.819 --> 00:03:11.370
Its public spending, for example, is half
of the US’s and a third of Sweden’s.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:16.510
Listen to the words of the historic president
of Singapore, Lee Kuan Yew, which successfully

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:21.540
reflect the real foundations of this country’s
economic success.

00:03:21.540 --> 00:03:27.350
“When most of the Third World was deeply
suspicious of exploitation by western multinational

00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:29.599
corporations, Singapore invited them in.

00:03:29.599 --> 00:03:33.889
They helped us grow, brought in technology
and know-how, and raised productivity levels

00:03:33.889 --> 00:03:36.870
faster than any alternative strategy could.

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:38.150
Lee Kuan Yew.)

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:42.099
Singapore’s success is therefore not due
to the financial industry but due to the same

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:48.209
reasons that have led to the growth of other
economies: legal security, low taxes, monetary

00:03:48.209 --> 00:03:51.200
stability and moderate regulations.

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:58.290
A commitment to the free market that has made
this country become, in many areas, an example

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.450
to follow.

00:04:00.450 --> 00:04:08.530
And we’re not only talking strictly about
economics, but also certain social issues,

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:09.530
such as health.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:16.340
Yes, you heard it right, Singapore is also
a role model in terms of health policy.

00:04:16.340 --> 00:04:23.060
(AN IMPOSSIBLE CHALLENGE?)

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:27.090
Let's take a look, for example, at Spain’s
case, a country whose health system is considered

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:28.669
one of the most efficient on the planet.

00:04:28.669 --> 00:04:32.160
Well, the problem is that, beyond being corrigible,
it isn’t sustainable.

00:04:32.160 --> 00:04:36.680
See, in just a decade, the total cost of the
Spanish health system has almost doubled,

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:40.590
and everything indicates that as a result
of the aging population and the demand for

00:04:40.590 --> 00:04:45.830
new and expensive health technologies, this
trend will be unstoppable, and will continue

00:04:45.830 --> 00:04:50.220
to worsen year after year...

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:53.940
And don’t think that this only happens in
Spain, not at all.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:57.810
Medical bills, that is the sustainability
of health systems is a widespread problem

00:04:57.810 --> 00:04:59.099
in other developed countries.

00:04:59.099 --> 00:05:04.770
So the question that arises is how can we
overcome these challenges and ensure the sustainability

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:08.639
of health systems?

00:05:08.639 --> 00:05:12.650
A good model – which is never taken into
account – could perhaps be that of a completely

00:05:12.650 --> 00:05:17.460
free health system, a system in which demand
and supply are determined by the market and

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:18.860
not in political offices.

00:05:18.860 --> 00:05:20.590
This could lead to some benefits:

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:25.090
For one, more money for families thanks to
lower taxes, and a precautionary saving level

00:05:25.090 --> 00:05:28.910
against any ailments that may arise that’s
higher than the present one.

00:05:28.910 --> 00:05:33.139
This would allow for direct primary care payments
and insurance against minor ailments, common

00:05:33.139 --> 00:05:41.600
issues, and more expensive treatments.

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.940
Another benefit would be a greater diversity
of and competition among different health

00:05:44.940 --> 00:05:49.169
centers, in such a way that, given the threat
of being displaced in the market, productivity,

00:05:49.169 --> 00:05:51.889
differentiation and innovation would be promoted.

00:05:51.889 --> 00:05:55.720
In other words, we’d achieve a wide range
of services that would positively impact both

00:05:55.720 --> 00:06:05.650
the quality of the service and lead to lower
prices.

00:06:05.650 --> 00:06:09.919
Competition is usually the best formula to
guarantee better goods and services for consumers.

00:06:09.919 --> 00:06:13.180
And health isn’t any different.

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:17.169
However, nowadays, no country uses all of
these principles.

00:06:17.169 --> 00:06:21.800
And no, the US health system, as we’ll see,
isn’t precisely a free market model.

00:06:21.800 --> 00:06:25.020
Now, there is a country that has used some
of these characteristics.

00:06:25.020 --> 00:06:27.560
And it has done so with extraordinary results.

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:34.979
Listen to Singapore’s case.

00:06:34.979 --> 00:06:40.280
(A MODEL FOR HEALTH REFORM)

00:06:40.280 --> 00:06:47.150
Right now, Singapore has the best healthcare
system in the world: it’s top quality and

00:06:47.150 --> 00:06:54.750
costs practically half that of Spain’s health
system, and less than a third of the US’s.

00:06:54.750 --> 00:07:01.889
But this isn’t just a matter of lower costs--it’s
also, and above all, about quality and results:

00:07:01.889 --> 00:07:06.159
See, life expectancy at birth in Singapore
is two to three years longer than in Britain

00:07:06.159 --> 00:07:07.540
or the United States.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.111
Its infant mortality rate is among the lowest
in the world and is almost half that of the

00:07:11.111 --> 00:07:15.810
United States, Great Britain, Australia, Canada
or France.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:21.500
And in general its mortality rates are impressive
compared to most Western countries.

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:26.150
Now... the question we can ask ourselves is
how they achieved such success?

00:07:26.150 --> 00:07:29.979
Well in this case they have achieved it, fundamentally,
by using market criteria.

00:07:29.979 --> 00:07:33.720
First of all, the main feature of the Singapore
model doesn’t just lie in the fact that

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:39.419
more than 60% of all health spending is private,
but that to a large extent, it is directly

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:40.439
paid by users.

00:07:40.439 --> 00:07:44.580
That is, co-payment is the norm here.

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:50.680
But, not everyone pays the same when they
go to a hospital.

00:07:50.680 --> 00:07:56.199
There are public and private centers, as well
as 5 levels, 5 classes: A, B1, B2 +, B2 and

00:07:56.199 --> 00:07:57.199
C.

00:07:57.199 --> 00:08:01.150
Each of these letters gives users a different
service: for example class A gets you a private

00:08:01.150 --> 00:08:05.270
room and a choice of doctor; while in class
C the room is shared and the doctor is assigned

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:06.270
by the hospital.

00:08:06.270 --> 00:08:08.430
Of course, each class has a different cost.

00:08:08.430 --> 00:08:13.590
If a user chooses class A, they pay for everything,
if instead they choose class C, the government

00:08:13.590 --> 00:08:17.360
subsidizes 80% of the cost.

00:08:17.360 --> 00:08:21.960
But where do people get the money to pay for
healthcare?

00:08:21.960 --> 00:08:26.540
Well from the same place as in other countries:
from the wealth they generate, from their

00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:27.540
work.

00:08:27.540 --> 00:08:30.870
The difference is that the huge contributions
that governments usually collect, are saved

00:08:30.870 --> 00:08:35.640
in Singapore-- in a mandatory way yes--in
special accounts under each citizen.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:40.190
They can only use these accounts for certain
purposes, such as paying their pensions, their

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:42.350
children’s education, homes or health.

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:46.230
In the case of health care, the account is
known as Medisave and is used to pay for doctor

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:51.600
visits, hospitalizations, medications, etc.,
etc.

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.380
This is the second key point of Singapore's
medical system

00:08:54.380 --> 00:08:58.580
Thanks to this formula, demand is self-regulated
and people can manage how to spend the money

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:02.880
they accumulate in these accounts – for
example by accessing different kinds of care,

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:07.280
whether they want to receive or be hospitalized
in hospitals with fewer comforts but which

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:12.020
are cheaper, or with more services and higher
costs – decreasing redundant and superfluous

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:14.650
consumption.

00:09:14.650 --> 00:09:18.910
Another very important point is that, for
the most serious cases, there’s a universal

00:09:18.910 --> 00:09:25.070
public insurance known as MediShield Life
that hardly costs $16 a month for a-30 year-old.

00:09:25.070 --> 00:09:29.400
And with it, of course, comes the possibility
of hiring other private insurers that improve

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:31.720
the services offered.

00:09:31.720 --> 00:09:37.090
Finally, the third leg of Singapore’s health
system is public expenditure itself:

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:39.950
What happens if someone can’t cover their
medical bills?

00:09:39.950 --> 00:09:44.200
Well, to guarantee access to health care,
the government subsidizes hospitals and has

00:09:44.200 --> 00:09:50.700
a public fund, the Medifund, designed to pay
health bills for lower-income families.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:58.110
So, folks, this is how Singapore has managed
to create a relatively economical medical

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:02.140
system, while achieving results and quality
levels that place it among the best health

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:05.910
systems in the world according to the World
Health Organization and different rankings

00:10:05.910 --> 00:10:11.510
such as Bloomberg’s "Most Efficient Health
Care Systems".

00:10:11.510 --> 00:10:16.270
However, despite all this, when we usually
talk about healthcare and the free market

00:10:16.270 --> 00:10:19.470
we automatically think of the United States
and not Singapore.

00:10:19.470 --> 00:10:23.680
And that is a serious mistake.

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:32.350
(USA vs. Singapore)

00:10:32.350 --> 00:10:39.090
Indeed, the US doesn’t have a European-style
public health system.

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:42.720
And since the country’s results are quite
deplorable, considering that it spends more

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:48.120
than 17% of its GDP on its healthcare system,
which is twice that of most European countries,

00:10:48.120 --> 00:10:52.350
the debate on health seems settled: private
health care is worse than public health care

00:10:52.350 --> 00:10:57.160
because it costs twice as much and has similar
or even lower service levels.

00:10:57.160 --> 00:11:01.790
But this, as we saw with Singapore, where
60% of health spending is private, isn’t

00:11:01.790 --> 00:11:02.790
true.

00:11:02.790 --> 00:11:05.620
In Singapore, the health care system isn’t
only better, it’s one of the best in the

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:11.930
world and involves much lower costs than in
other developed countries.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:16.380
The issue is that the US health system isn’t
only heavily intervened upon and regulated

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:20.830
by the government, its cost is tremendously
socialized.

00:11:20.830 --> 00:11:27.200
See, 90% of all health spending is channeled
through two different agents other than the

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:28.200
patient.

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:32.640
Out of every 100 dollars spent in the US health
system, 45 come from insurance companies,

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:42.100
45 from state programs such as Medicaid, and
only 10 from patients’ pockets.

00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:49.370
Yes, public health spending in the United
States is in relative terms higher, for example,

00:11:49.370 --> 00:11:50.400
than in Spain.

00:11:50.400 --> 00:11:52.400
But we’ll talk about that in another video...

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.720
The fact is that as we’ve seen, Singapore
sets a healthcare system model on the table

00:11:56.720 --> 00:12:00.920
that has a great combination of cost, innovation
and medical results.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:04.680
We may like some aspects of this system, and
not others… but I think it wouldn’t be

00:12:04.680 --> 00:12:10.660
unreasonable to take it into account when
talking about health, the future, and sustainability.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:14.410
So I really hope you enjoyed this video, please
hit like if you did, and don’t forget to

